WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.908 --> 00:00:10.110
So today, we're going to have
an introduction to big data.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:13.037
So can you put your hands
up, in the audience--

00:00:13.037 --> 00:00:15.620
and I'm going to do my best to
have a look-- if you're working

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:18.181
with big data at the moment.

00:00:18.181 --> 00:00:18.680
OK.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.950
So that's about a
third of the room.

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:21.800
And I'm blind.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:23.420
There we go.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.170
All right, so I
have an impression.

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:28.910
So a little story about
big data to start with.

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:32.156
You probably all know
Google Street View.

00:00:32.156 --> 00:00:33.530
So when we were
having these cars

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:36.589
driving around taking pictures
so we could have this nice--

00:00:36.589 --> 00:00:39.130
there's a great story about how
Street View actually started.

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:40.500
I'll save that for another day.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.040
But if you Google it and
Google Larry in Street View

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.860
and how it all started, I'm
sure you'll find a nice story.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:48.110
When we were doing that, we
were just basically trying

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:49.730
to capture those
images so we could

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:51.620
create this nice experience.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:53.870
What we didn't really
think about at the time

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:56.570
was how much data we
were actually capturing.

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:59.310
So I think I'm going to
move on to the next slide.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:02.720
And here, we're going to look
at what kind of information

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.610
is already in that image.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:07.250
And, obviously, in the
years after Street View,

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:10.730
when we started to supply
information on which way--

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:13.610
with traffic and
things like that,

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:16.267
which signs, where the
addresses are, what's going on.

00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:17.850
So there's all this
information there.

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.260
So this is quite
a rich data set.

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:22.280
And you can imagine that
if you have this data,

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.740
you can already start to do
something very useful with it

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:26.900
if you can somehow get
to the information that's

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:28.130
stored in there.

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:30.740
So that's a little
story to start with.

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:33.620
So big data applications.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:38.090
Some of them involve a
human, and others don't.

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:39.090
What does that mean?

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.210
So let's think about
an application that

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:43.730
involves a human, for
example, if you're

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:46.070
running some kind of
experiment and you're

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:47.950
trying to see what users
are actually doing.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:50.210
So you say, well, you know,
if we get this feature,

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.210
do we actually you get
some response from that?

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:54.450
Is it proving to be successful?

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:56.810
And so you're actually
looking at their interaction.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:00.290
Others might not necessarily
involve human interaction.

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:05.390
Might be, for example, blocking
fraudulent transactions.

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:08.535
So, for example, we're looking
at credit cards coming in,

00:02:08.535 --> 00:02:10.910
transactions, and we want to
know which ones are actually

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:13.760
valid or fraudulent.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.470
So we might build a
model or something,

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:16.710
and actually start
processing that data.

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:18.085
And you can imagine
how much data

00:02:18.085 --> 00:02:21.200
you might have coming at you
as a bank with all these credit

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.430
card transactions
going on all the time.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.210
So there's a couple of examples
of big data applications,

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:29.180
or, let's say, use cases.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:32.390
Today, we're going to actually
look at four different tools

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:37.250
that we can use from
Google Cloud's Suite which

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:40.140
will actually help us do
something with big data.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.330
So we're going to look
at how we analyze data.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:44.720
We're going to look at
how we can prepare data.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:47.067
We're going to look at
how we can process it.

00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:49.400
We're actually going to look
at some of the end results,

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:51.090
how we can actually
start using that

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:55.370
in dashboarding, for example,
and also how we might actually

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:57.504
leverage the skills
of a data scientist

00:02:57.504 --> 00:02:59.420
to actually interact
with that data to create,

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:02.180
for example, a machine
learning model.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:06.091
So this is a little something
to think about, about big data

00:03:06.091 --> 00:03:06.590
as well.

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:08.756
Because when you talk to
people out there, they say,

00:03:08.756 --> 00:03:12.290
well, I'm not doing
anything with big data.

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:13.530
I have a database.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.960
There's a terabyte of
information in there.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.390
It's not a lot.

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:19.999
But then, let's have a think,
like, what kind of data

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:21.540
do you have, actually,
at the moment.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.780
So this is the data that
you analyze now, today,

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.284
and that data is probably,
like I said, in a database.

00:03:26.284 --> 00:03:28.450
It might be up to a terabyte
or something like that.

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:30.240
It's well structured,
quite well.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.990
Your business intelligence
teams are probably

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:34.490
working with it quite a lot.

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:37.360
So you've got a good grip
on what that actually is.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.140
There could be data
that you collect,

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:41.760
but you don't actually
process or analyze.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.600
So you could be collecting logs
from your website application,

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:47.750
for example, server logs, which
are telling you what things are

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:49.910
happening, what users
are clicking on,

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:52.674
what page users start
off on, and where

00:03:52.674 --> 00:03:54.840
they move to throughout
that journey on the website.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.330
But you might not be
processing that, for example.

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:59.000
And then there's data that
you're not even collecting.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:00.260
So maybe in those
logs, you're only

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:02.509
collecting at a certain
level, and you're not actually

00:04:02.509 --> 00:04:05.000
keeping all of the
information because it's

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.790
too expensive to
store or something.

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:09.800
And then also, on
top of that, there's

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:12.060
data you can actually
acquire from third parties.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:15.350
So what would be a
good case in this?

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:18.350
So let's say you're doing
something with recommendations,

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:21.079
and you want to get some
information about seasonality,

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:22.930
what's happening with the
weather, is there a correlation

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:23.350
there?

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:25.100
You're probably not going
to go and stick sensors

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:26.099
up all around the world.

00:04:26.099 --> 00:04:27.690
Someone else is
doing that for you.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:30.500
So you probably buy that data,
find some way of acquiring it,

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:32.440
so you can bring it
into your ecosystem

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:34.190
to start doing this thing.

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:36.071
As going back to that
first image, when

00:04:36.071 --> 00:04:38.570
we took those photos, there was
already a lot of information

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:39.110
there.

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:41.032
Once you find out the
use cases and start

00:04:41.032 --> 00:04:42.490
looking at what
data could be there

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:44.150
and how you could
discover that, then you

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:45.983
can think more about
what you might actually

00:04:45.983 --> 00:04:49.040
want to do with that data.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:51.520
So we're going to start off
with some of the challenges.

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:55.280
And the first one is how to
develop big data pipelines.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:58.630
So we're going to talk
about a UI application

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:01.320
that we've created called
Google Cloud Dataprep.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.310
It was launched this year.

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:10.210
And it's a tool that basically
allows your analysts and data

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:15.700
scientists to actually interact
with raw data, to clean it up,

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:18.640
to process it, and, actually,
to get it into a database

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:20.720
where they can
start analyzing it.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.570
So one of the challenges--

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:26.080
before I joined Google, I headed
up a business intelligence team

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:27.135
for a number of years.

00:05:27.135 --> 00:05:29.260
And one of the challenges
that the team would have,

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:30.759
especially the
analysts on the team,

00:05:30.759 --> 00:05:32.890
was when we didn't have
enough development capacity

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:34.232
to build pipelines.

00:05:34.232 --> 00:05:36.440
Some of the analysts and
some of the data scientists,

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:39.070
they wanted to start
experimenting, creating models,

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:40.900
and coming up with
some information

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:43.860
to tell their
business users what

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:46.110
was going on with the products
and that kind of stuff.

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:49.340
But because we hadn't
processed that data yet,

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:51.400
they won't be able to
make those insights.

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:52.840
So having some
kind of tool where

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:55.300
they can kind of self service,
where they can actually

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:59.020
go in there and process that--
look at that data, process it.

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:01.422
And key to this is that
once they've actually

00:06:01.422 --> 00:06:03.130
had a look at that
data and played around

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:05.360
with a sample of it,
whatever we create there

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:07.060
that we can actually
apply at scale

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:10.390
and use it to process all
that data that they might want

00:06:10.390 --> 00:06:13.150
to use in the data warehouse
for their next steps,

00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:16.010
or the analysis, or
the data science.

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:20.730
So we're going to look
at two data sets, one

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:23.320
that the data set at the
bottom is ad impressions.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:26.780
And this is basically some
data stored in the CSV file.

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:31.900
And as the red highlight
is highlighting,

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:33.700
we want to extract
some information there

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:35.690
which is the product ID.

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:37.810
So that is something you
might see on a web URL.

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:39.934
Someone goes to a page and
the product ID is there.

00:06:39.934 --> 00:06:42.064
But we need to get at that ID.

00:06:42.064 --> 00:06:43.480
The other thing
that we want to do

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:45.880
is we want to look
at some transactions.

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:47.897
And these transactions
are restoring a BigQuery.

00:06:47.897 --> 00:06:49.480
And if you're
interested in BigQuery--

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:51.450
I'll probably say this again
somewhere later in the talk--

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:52.330
but if you're
interested in BigQuery,

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:54.195
stick around for the
next talk because it's

00:06:54.195 --> 00:06:55.925
when I was going to give us
a great talk about BigQuery.

00:06:55.925 --> 00:06:58.240
So we will only be touching
on that very lightly

00:06:58.240 --> 00:06:59.230
during this talk.

00:06:59.230 --> 00:07:02.290
But in BigQuery, in this
particular data set,

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:04.210
we have some transactions.

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:06.160
And what's actually
quite special

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:09.970
about these transactions is
that we're storing, in BigQuery

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:11.307
itself, nested data.

00:07:11.307 --> 00:07:13.390
So one of the things that
you can do with BigQuery

00:07:13.390 --> 00:07:15.640
is not just store
your flat table,

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:18.170
but you can actually have,
within each row, a collection.

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:20.560
So think about order and
order lines, or a transaction

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:22.160
and transaction lines.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:25.169
So we have a transaction
with the different lines,

00:07:25.169 --> 00:07:27.460
with the transaction lines,
and we want to get to all--

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:29.140
and at the transaction
line level,

00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:30.880
we're storing the product key.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:32.330
And that's what
we want to get at.

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:34.871
So what we're basically going
to try and do in this data set,

00:07:34.871 --> 00:07:37.110
we're going to try to
join the two together.

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:38.710
And we want to
create one data set

00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:42.670
where we can look at which
ad impressions actually

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:44.159
resulted in the transaction.

00:07:44.159 --> 00:07:45.700
And maybe we want
to look at the time

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:47.560
between those two or something.

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:49.960
So we're going to attempt
to do this now in a demo

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:51.710
without actually
writing any code.

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:54.250
So that's the goal of Dataprep.

00:07:54.250 --> 00:07:56.262
So we're going to
switch over to a demo.

00:07:56.262 --> 00:07:57.220
This is Cloud Dataprep.

00:07:57.220 --> 00:08:01.150
It's accessible via
the cloud console.

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:02.530
And we're going to start off--

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:04.490
I've imported two data
sets to save time.

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:08.560
The top one is the CSV,
and this other data

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:13.180
set here, the scale 2, is
our data set from BigQuery.

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:15.490
So we're going to
go to flows here.

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:17.660
And here is where you
basically create and you flow.

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:19.420
So once you've imported
those data sets,

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:22.680
here is when you're going
to start working with them.

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:27.020
So we're going to
create a new flow demo.

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:27.700
Please be kind.

00:08:32.164 --> 00:08:34.080
We're going to add a
couple of data sets here.

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:36.260
So I'm adding the
CSV, and I'm adding

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:38.650
the sales scaled dataset.

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:47.710
So here is the sales scaled
that's coming from BigQuery,

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:49.060
and here's a CSV.

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:51.610
And you can already see
here, I dropped the CSV,

00:08:51.610 --> 00:08:54.110
but some action has
already occurred.

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:57.490
So basically what Dataprep
is doing is creating recipes.

00:08:57.490 --> 00:08:59.470
And those recipes
is basically what

00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:03.370
you need to do to cook your
data from raw to something--

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:05.710
well, cooked-- that
you can start using.

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:07.930
So let's have a look at
what is already suggested.

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:09.170
Well, it was a CSV.

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:10.890
So it understood
that it was a CSV.

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:12.700
So it looked at the delimiter.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:15.330
So there's a certain amount
of intelligence and machine

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:18.190
learning behind this to
actually do some of this stuff.

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:21.617
And you'll see in a moment how
powerful some of that can be.

00:09:21.617 --> 00:09:23.200
It's already come
up with a suggestion

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:24.450
you can see here on the right.

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:27.770
So it's actually worked out
the type of columns that it is.

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:30.190
Let's go and have it edit
our recipe for a moment.

00:09:33.039 --> 00:09:34.830
Keeping an eye on the
time in the meantime.

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:44.730
There we go.

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:46.910
So what do we see here?

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:50.204
We've basically got all of
the columns extracted for us.

00:09:50.204 --> 00:09:51.620
You can see, for
each column, it's

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:53.340
already suggested a data type.

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:54.860
So this one is alphanumeric.

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:56.330
This one is a number.

00:09:56.330 --> 00:09:57.950
And here we have a data/time.

00:09:57.950 --> 00:10:00.990
And we've got the
distribution of the data.

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:03.610
So basically, the
data set I imported

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:06.570
was a CSV in a Cloud
Storage bucket.

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:09.100
If there had been
multiple CSVs there,

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:12.290
it would basically be able
to import all of those.

00:10:12.290 --> 00:10:15.170
And you see at the top here,
this is quite a small data set.

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:16.850
So it's just picking
the whole data set

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:18.760
because that fits quite nicely.

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:20.470
But if it wasn't,
you could actually--

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:23.200
if it was a bigger data set, we
could collect a random sample.

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:25.220
So we use different
sampling techniques

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:27.140
to actually have a
look at that data,

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:30.380
but since we have all of it
here, that's easy enough.

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:33.170
So we can see here the
distribution of the data.

00:10:33.170 --> 00:10:36.170
And it's going into
different columns.

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:39.150
And one of the columns here,
the event time, if I look here,

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:43.760
I can see that there's some data
which is actually a little bit

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:45.110
problematic.

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:47.020
What is it saying.

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:48.470
Let's have a look.

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:52.870
So you notice, all I did
was click here on that data.

00:10:52.870 --> 00:10:54.620
So I just clicked on
that little red mark.

00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:57.110
So Dataprep is really
the kind of GUI

00:10:57.110 --> 00:10:59.110
where you have to
just click around,

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:01.160
and it kind of works
out what you're doing.

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:02.990
So I clicked here
on this red part

00:11:02.990 --> 00:11:05.510
because that looked odd
to me, and it came up

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:06.290
with a suggestion.

