00:21:45.780 --> 00:21:47.370
So I mentioned
Dataflow a few times

00:21:47.370 --> 00:21:50.510
in that last presentation,
that it will--

00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:52.290
the Dataprep itself
will actually

00:21:52.290 --> 00:21:54.540
create a Dataflow
job which is going

00:21:54.540 --> 00:21:57.040
to run to process your data.

00:21:57.040 --> 00:21:58.200
So what is Dataflow?

00:21:58.200 --> 00:22:01.110
And we're going to take
a use case of streaming.

00:22:01.110 --> 00:22:05.460
So a few things about streaming.

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:08.700
A lot of the good systems that
are out there were built to--

00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:11.760
they were designed to run
on distribution systems.

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:13.350
They weren't
necessarily designed

00:22:13.350 --> 00:22:15.440
to be very friendly
for developers.

00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:17.834
I mean Map produces
great, And Google,

00:22:17.834 --> 00:22:19.500
we published a white
paper back on that,

00:22:19.500 --> 00:22:23.160
produced somewhere early 2000.

00:22:23.160 --> 00:22:26.430
It's a fundamental part
of a lot of the products

00:22:26.430 --> 00:22:27.420
that we make at Google.

00:22:27.420 --> 00:22:31.990
Also, Dataflow is also behind
BigQuery a certain level.

00:22:31.990 --> 00:22:36.840
So it's a very important,
let's say, programming model.

00:22:36.840 --> 00:22:39.312
But it's quite difficult as
a developer, especially when

00:22:39.312 --> 00:22:41.520
you're working on small
tasks, or for the first time,

00:22:41.520 --> 00:22:42.960
to get your head around.

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:45.180
And then we have some
additional problems.

00:22:45.180 --> 00:22:47.110
We have batch, which
we just looked at,

00:22:47.110 --> 00:22:49.690
which is really nice when
everything is already there,

00:22:49.690 --> 00:22:51.540
and you can actually
take it, move on,

00:22:51.540 --> 00:22:53.790
and you know the whole data
set that you're looking at

00:22:53.790 --> 00:22:54.750
is complete.

00:22:54.750 --> 00:22:56.510
So nothing is going
to come at you later.

00:22:56.510 --> 00:23:00.210
You look at data that arrives
tomorrow because it's a batch.

00:23:00.210 --> 00:23:01.410
It's a complete set.

00:23:01.410 --> 00:23:03.900
So you know you're looking
at the whole picture.

00:23:03.900 --> 00:23:06.600
In the case of
streaming, that's not.

00:23:06.600 --> 00:23:08.100
You're just getting
single events,

00:23:08.100 --> 00:23:10.890
or, let's say, little groups
of events coming at you.

00:23:10.890 --> 00:23:13.140
And you don't actually know
if you've seen everything.

00:23:13.140 --> 00:23:14.598
You don't even know
if everything's

00:23:14.598 --> 00:23:15.490
in the right order.

00:23:15.490 --> 00:23:17.864
So there's this whole notion
of processing time and event

00:23:17.864 --> 00:23:21.100
time that can be quite difficult
to wrap your heads around.

00:23:21.100 --> 00:23:24.720
So the first key
component in this--

00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:28.062
and I actually should have
put a arctic slide in here--

00:23:28.062 --> 00:23:29.520
the first key
component is actually

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:30.520
getting those events in.

00:23:30.520 --> 00:23:33.840
So one of the things that
we're going to use is Pub/Sub.

00:23:33.840 --> 00:23:36.860
Pub/Sub is basically
message queuing.

00:23:36.860 --> 00:23:38.730
It's a global system.

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:42.870
You can send literally
millions of messages per second

00:23:42.870 --> 00:23:45.410
to the system if you need to.

00:23:45.410 --> 00:23:47.640
It will guarantee you
at least once delivery

00:23:47.640 --> 00:23:48.700
on any subscriber.

00:23:48.700 --> 00:23:50.884
So it's called Pub/Sub,
so publish and subscribe.

00:23:50.884 --> 00:23:52.800
So you have a publisher,
and you have a topic.

00:23:52.800 --> 00:23:55.500
So your publisher is sending
this thing to a given topic.

00:23:55.500 --> 00:23:57.300
And all your subscribers
on that topic--

00:23:57.300 --> 00:23:58.910
you can have as many
as you want as well--

00:23:58.910 --> 00:24:00.368
will sit there and
listen, and they

00:24:00.368 --> 00:24:04.330
will receive messages, either
by a push or a pull mechanism.

00:24:04.330 --> 00:24:05.092
And this is like--

00:24:05.092 --> 00:24:07.050
think of if you're going
to create any pipeline

00:24:07.050 --> 00:24:08.130
down stream, where
you're actually

00:24:08.130 --> 00:24:09.750
going to be handling
streaming data,

00:24:09.750 --> 00:24:12.124
Pub/Sub is a good place to
actually start buffering that.

00:24:12.124 --> 00:24:14.820
Because if you turn that system
off, or it's not running,

00:24:14.820 --> 00:24:17.005
you still need to be
able to get at the data.

00:24:17.005 --> 00:24:18.510
Or when you want
to start up again,

00:24:18.510 --> 00:24:21.470
you want to be able
to pick off the data

00:24:21.470 --> 00:24:22.830
that you haven't yet processed.

00:24:22.830 --> 00:24:26.730
So having a buffer in
between is very important.

00:24:26.730 --> 00:24:29.080
A little bit about the
event processing time.

00:24:29.080 --> 00:24:31.987
So in an ideal world, we would
see that diagonal line there,

00:24:31.987 --> 00:24:32.820
that diagonal slope.

00:24:32.820 --> 00:24:34.650
That would be perfect, you know.

00:24:34.650 --> 00:24:38.010
Every time something happens,
we can process it instantly.

00:24:38.010 --> 00:24:39.750
In reality, this
is not the case.

00:24:39.750 --> 00:24:42.580
I will give an example now.

00:24:42.580 --> 00:24:45.480
So in this example-- before I
explain what Apache Beam is--

00:24:45.480 --> 00:24:48.660
in this example here, we have
event, a time on the x-axis

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:49.320
and the y-axis.

00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:51.540
We have processing time.

00:24:51.540 --> 00:24:54.567
And again, we have the dotted
line, the diagonal line,

00:24:54.567 --> 00:24:55.650
which is the perfect line.

00:24:55.650 --> 00:24:58.960
So number three is almost
in the perfect line.

00:24:58.960 --> 00:25:01.664
Imagine these numbers
represent scores in a game.

00:25:01.664 --> 00:25:04.080
And the only thing that we're
actually interested in doing

00:25:04.080 --> 00:25:06.240
is, for this particular user--

00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:08.630
and let's just say all this
data is from one user--

00:25:08.630 --> 00:25:11.610
we are interested in
actually calculating a score

00:25:11.610 --> 00:25:14.565
and sending it back
to him, or putting it

00:25:14.565 --> 00:25:16.115
on a leaderboard somewhere.

00:25:16.115 --> 00:25:18.240
So every time we get an
update with how many points

00:25:18.240 --> 00:25:21.000
he's scored, so we
want to better process

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:23.880
that and represent
near real time,

00:25:23.880 --> 00:25:26.570
on a dashboard, his score.

00:25:26.570 --> 00:25:27.940
So three is really nice.

00:25:27.940 --> 00:25:28.980
The event time and
the processing time

00:25:28.980 --> 00:25:30.340
are very close to each other.

00:25:30.340 --> 00:25:33.180
This doesn't really
happen very often.

00:25:33.180 --> 00:25:35.040
what we do with
Dataflow, is we try

00:25:35.040 --> 00:25:37.800
to create a heuristic
watermark, which

00:25:37.800 --> 00:25:41.250
represents this kind of
relationship between the event

00:25:41.250 --> 00:25:42.840
time and the processing time.

00:25:42.840 --> 00:25:45.014
But it's based on the
information that it receives.

00:25:45.014 --> 00:25:46.680
And it's not going
to be based on things

00:25:46.680 --> 00:25:50.040
like if something comes
much later than I expected.

00:25:50.040 --> 00:25:52.710
So score 9 here
came really late.

00:25:52.710 --> 00:25:55.440
It happened just a
little bit after 12:01,

00:25:55.440 --> 00:25:57.630
and we didn't process
it until 12:08.

00:25:57.630 --> 00:25:58.910
So it came really late.

00:25:58.910 --> 00:26:01.320
In fact, many different
scores that we received,

00:26:01.320 --> 00:26:04.890
all the way up to this
score here, in the meantime,

00:26:04.890 --> 00:26:06.690
we actually processed
those before we even

00:26:06.690 --> 00:26:08.040
saw this one come in.

00:26:08.040 --> 00:26:09.870
So that's problematic.

00:26:09.870 --> 00:26:11.330
What we've done here--

00:26:11.330 --> 00:26:15.120
so Apache Beam is the open
source project for Dataflow.

00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:18.390
Dataflow is our managed
service to run Apache Beam.

00:26:18.390 --> 00:26:21.330
So much like TensorFlow is an
open source project for machine

00:26:21.330 --> 00:26:24.810
learning, and you can use Cloud
ML to run your machine learning

00:26:24.810 --> 00:26:28.520
projects, do your training, or
to do your predictions there,

00:26:28.520 --> 00:26:30.380
Dataflow will give
you the same ability

00:26:30.380 --> 00:26:33.060
to take your Apache
Beam processing.

00:26:33.060 --> 00:26:34.490
So it's a map produced like.

00:26:34.490 --> 00:26:36.230
But the important
thing about Apache Beam

00:26:36.230 --> 00:26:38.047
is unified batch and stream.

00:26:38.047 --> 00:26:39.880
And what does unified
batch and stream mean?

00:26:39.880 --> 00:26:41.510
Basically, we're
giving the semantics

00:26:41.510 --> 00:26:43.460
to be able to deal with data,
regardless of whether it's

00:26:43.460 --> 00:26:44.126
batch or stream.

00:26:44.126 --> 00:26:46.670
So you don't need to go and
create two different pipelines.

00:26:46.670 --> 00:26:48.794
And then the important
part, being able to do that,

00:26:48.794 --> 00:26:50.850
is to be able to, first
of all, window your data.

00:26:50.850 --> 00:26:53.660
So do you want to window in
fixed windows, in session time.

00:26:53.660 --> 00:26:57.770
Do you want to window in
sliding windows, for example?

00:26:57.770 --> 00:26:59.570
And also to be able
to deal with data

00:26:59.570 --> 00:27:01.280
as it arrives late or early.

00:27:01.280 --> 00:27:03.770
And in this particular
case here of nine,

00:27:03.770 --> 00:27:06.440
we are dealing with a piece
of data that arrives late.

00:27:06.440 --> 00:27:08.180
So that data comes in late.

00:27:08.180 --> 00:27:10.544
We fire a trigger and
say, it's come in late.

00:27:10.544 --> 00:27:12.210
And in that program,
it will do as well,

00:27:12.210 --> 00:27:15.890
we also tell it, in our window,
if data comes at us late,

00:27:15.890 --> 00:27:18.650
we would also like to
still be able to keep

00:27:18.650 --> 00:27:21.117
all the data we have in the
window, so we can accumulate.

00:27:21.117 --> 00:27:22.700
And then we will add
that, and we will

00:27:22.700 --> 00:27:25.280
admit an update of the score.

00:27:25.280 --> 00:27:26.970
So you don't have
to do it like that.

00:27:26.970 --> 00:27:30.320
But that's just an example of
how you might want to do it.

00:27:30.320 --> 00:27:31.070
So let's move on.

00:27:32.247 --> 00:27:34.980
So for anyone has visited the
booth on the Instant Insights,

00:27:34.980 --> 00:27:37.095
this might already
be familiar to you.

00:27:37.095 --> 00:27:39.220
This is the architecture
I was just speaking about,

00:27:39.220 --> 00:27:40.170
at the beginning.

00:27:40.170 --> 00:27:40.790
So a Pub/Sub.

00:27:40.790 --> 00:27:42.040
So we've got events happening.

00:27:42.040 --> 00:27:43.760
Here, taxis are driving around.

00:27:43.760 --> 00:27:46.477
And we're going to use data flow
to process that information.

00:27:46.477 --> 00:27:48.060
And as that information
is processing,

00:27:48.060 --> 00:27:49.351
we're going to do three things.

00:27:49.351 --> 00:27:52.940
So all the solid arrows here
are actually streaming lines,

00:27:52.940 --> 00:27:55.610
and all the dashed arrows,
these are batch lines.

00:27:55.610 --> 00:27:58.220
So there are three things
going on in this pipeline.

00:27:58.220 --> 00:28:00.830
The first one is we're
taking all the data that

00:28:00.830 --> 00:28:02.780
comes at us, all the
events that we receive,

00:28:02.780 --> 00:28:04.613
and we're sending them
straight to BigQuery,

00:28:04.613 --> 00:28:06.260
so we can analyze them.

00:28:06.260 --> 00:28:08.980
By doing that, all the data that
we already have in BigQuery--

00:28:08.980 --> 00:28:11.360
so our data warehouse--

00:28:11.360 --> 00:28:14.960
all the data that we already
there for this particular table

00:28:14.960 --> 00:28:17.720
and all the data that's being
streamed in, we can query.

00:28:17.720 --> 00:28:21.350
So we have the possibilities
to do real time querying

00:28:21.350 --> 00:28:23.992
on the data and the historical
data, as it comes in.

00:28:23.992 --> 00:28:26.450
And that's good because then
we can use something like data

00:28:26.450 --> 00:28:27.730
prep to analyze the data.

00:28:27.730 --> 00:28:29.540
We'll look at some
other stuff in a moment.

00:28:29.540 --> 00:28:30.956
And once we've
done that, we could

00:28:30.956 --> 00:28:33.560
use that data, for example,
to retrain our model.

00:28:33.560 --> 00:28:36.500
And here, we would train a
model with intent to flow in

00:28:36.500 --> 00:28:38.930
and Cloud ML-- the
service I just mentioned.

00:28:38.930 --> 00:28:42.220
And once that model's
ready and been trained,

00:28:42.220 --> 00:28:45.980
we could release a new
version to our serving part

00:28:45.980 --> 00:28:47.170
of the Cloud ML space.

00:28:47.170 --> 00:28:48.920
And you can have
different versions there,

00:28:48.920 --> 00:28:51.572
which enables you to do
like AB testing on models.

00:28:51.572 --> 00:28:53.780
And what we're going to do
in this particular example

00:28:53.780 --> 00:28:57.410
is we're going to make a
prediction on how likely

00:28:57.410 --> 00:28:59.420
the users in that taxi--

00:28:59.420 --> 00:29:01.110
so what information do we have?

00:29:01.110 --> 00:29:04.710
We have where the trip
started, where the trip ended,

00:29:04.710 --> 00:29:07.670
how long the trip is taking so
far, and the number of people

00:29:07.670 --> 00:29:10.680
that are in the taxi itself.

00:29:10.680 --> 00:29:12.710
And we'll make
predictions on that,

00:29:12.710 --> 00:29:15.770
on how likely they are to
respond to a given advert.

00:29:15.770 --> 00:29:17.775
So one of those
adverse might be--

00:29:17.775 --> 00:29:19.610
and you can see
the example, if you

00:29:19.610 --> 00:29:21.320
want to see more of
this at the booth.

00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:23.480
But one of these examples
might be three people,

00:29:23.480 --> 00:29:26.870
driving in a taxi to the airport
might be interested in having

00:29:26.870 --> 00:29:30.000
sushi before you fly.

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:33.350
I'm not taking responsibility
for the adverts.

00:29:33.350 --> 00:29:36.050
Another example might be
someone driving from the airport

00:29:36.050 --> 00:29:38.210
into town, as a single
person, and they

00:29:38.210 --> 00:29:40.910
want to say, well, would
you like to visit this wine

00:29:40.910 --> 00:29:43.161
bar because we know
this wine bar is very

00:29:43.161 --> 00:29:44.660
popular with single
people that just

00:29:44.660 --> 00:29:47.130
come into town, for example.

00:29:47.130 --> 00:29:49.340
So that's the second
leg of this pipeline.

00:29:49.340 --> 00:29:52.430
Now we have this information
still coming in here.

00:29:52.430 --> 00:29:54.590
So we've sent a copy
of that information

00:29:54.590 --> 00:29:57.410
to the data warehouse and
we're sending another copy

00:29:57.410 --> 00:29:59.090
of that information--

00:29:59.090 --> 00:30:01.551
we're calling the data
here to make a prediction.

00:30:01.551 --> 00:30:03.050
And once we've made
that prediction,

00:30:03.050 --> 00:30:05.870
we will send them
the recommendations

00:30:05.870 --> 00:30:06.662
for the adverts.

00:30:06.662 --> 00:30:08.120
And the last thing
that we're doing

00:30:08.120 --> 00:30:10.400
is we're sending a small
aggregate of that data--

00:30:10.400 --> 00:30:12.108
so we're aggregating
that data comes in--

00:30:12.108 --> 00:30:13.800
so we can produce a heat map.

00:30:13.800 --> 00:30:15.680
So think about a
dashboard that you

00:30:15.680 --> 00:30:18.050
might have at the control
center to kind of show what's

00:30:18.050 --> 00:30:19.320
going on at the moment--

00:30:19.320 --> 00:30:21.320
how many taxis are driving
around at the moment?

00:30:21.320 --> 00:30:23.520
Which locations are
they driving around in?

00:30:23.520 --> 00:30:26.720
If you're having a conversation
with a potential client,

00:30:26.720 --> 00:30:28.190
you can say, well,
look, this area

00:30:28.190 --> 00:30:29.930
is really popular with taxis.

00:30:29.930 --> 00:30:31.360
Would you like to have--

00:30:31.360 --> 00:30:32.860
it could be a way
of actually trying

00:30:32.860 --> 00:30:35.232
to promote your
advertising space in there.

00:30:35.232 --> 00:30:37.190
So these adverts would
be, obviously, displayed

00:30:37.190 --> 00:30:39.680
on a little screen in
the back of the taxi,

00:30:39.680 --> 00:30:40.810
as they're driving around.

00:30:40.810 --> 00:30:43.746
It's a very modern taxi.

00:30:43.746 --> 00:30:45.620
So I'm going to just
show you a Dataflow very

00:30:45.620 --> 00:30:48.950
briefly because I've got
to speed up a little bit.

00:30:48.950 --> 00:30:53.160
It's not like me
to talk over time.

00:30:53.160 --> 00:30:54.520
So this is the same pipeline.

00:30:54.520 --> 00:30:56.330
So I'll just show you
a few features here.

00:30:56.330 --> 00:30:58.640
One, this is the data
coming from Pub/Sub.

00:30:58.640 --> 00:31:00.200
It's being read from Pub/Sub.

00:31:00.200 --> 00:31:01.580
And we're sending
it to BigQuery.

00:31:01.580 --> 00:31:03.020
And you can see, this is
running at the moment,

00:31:03.020 --> 00:31:04.853
so, again, go to the
demo booth, if you want

00:31:04.853 --> 00:31:06.950
to get more details on this.

00:31:06.950 --> 00:31:09.562
And the other branch I want to
show you here is the ML call.

00:31:09.562 --> 00:31:12.020
And one of the useful things
that you might want to do here

00:31:12.020 --> 00:31:13.900
is just open up the logs.

00:31:13.900 --> 00:31:17.470
And I've just
selected the ML call.

00:31:17.470 --> 00:31:20.470
I'll just give it a second
for this to populate.

00:31:20.470 --> 00:31:24.310
And you can see here that
there are requests being made

00:31:24.310 --> 00:31:26.230
and there are
responses being given

00:31:26.230 --> 00:31:28.370
for the different predictions.

00:31:28.370 --> 00:31:30.910
So this is online, it's
running at the moment.

00:31:30.910 --> 00:31:34.610
And please, have a look.

00:31:34.610 --> 00:31:36.950
So that was Dataflow.

00:31:36.950 --> 00:31:40.860
So we just looked about hard
to develop data pipelines.

00:31:40.860 --> 00:31:42.699
A good thing just to
reinforce-- everything

00:31:42.699 --> 00:31:44.990
that we've looked at the
moment is complete in managed.

00:31:44.990 --> 00:31:47.510
There are no computers for
you to manage yourself.

00:31:47.510 --> 00:31:49.280
If you run something
in Dataflow and you

00:31:49.280 --> 00:31:52.105
put the right configuration--
like you use as many machines

00:31:52.105 --> 00:31:53.480
as you need to--
it will scale up

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:55.670
and scale down to do
whatever it needs to do,

00:31:55.670 --> 00:31:57.720
to get the job done.

00:31:57.720 --> 00:32:00.140
The same with
BigQuery-- the data

00:32:00.140 --> 00:32:04.190
prep is creating a Dataflow
pipeline in the background.

00:32:04.190 --> 00:32:06.710
And Pub/Sub is a
completely managed service.

00:32:06.710 --> 00:32:09.800
And you're just not concerned
by any number of machines

00:32:09.800 --> 00:32:13.590
or anything there, it's just
the throughput that you want.

00:32:13.590 --> 00:32:17.720
So this next part is about
hard to scale and operate

00:32:17.720 --> 00:32:19.050
on Big Data applications.

00:32:19.050 --> 00:32:21.030
So let's have a look at
some of the challenges.

00:32:21.030 --> 00:32:22.405
These are some of
the challenges.

00:32:22.405 --> 00:32:24.000
I'm not going to
read them all not,

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:25.875
but, basically, this is
where you want to be.

00:32:25.875 --> 00:32:28.495
You want to spend your time
on insights and analytics.

00:32:28.495 --> 00:32:29.870
You don't want to
spend your time

00:32:29.870 --> 00:32:31.850
on doing performance
tuning, resource

00:32:31.850 --> 00:32:34.170
provisioning-- all that stuff.

00:32:34.170 --> 00:32:37.026
So let's move on.

00:32:37.026 --> 00:32:38.900
Let's look at those
three applications again.

00:32:38.900 --> 00:32:41.780
I just, basically,
foretold this slide.

00:32:41.780 --> 00:32:45.830
But Pub/Sub for ingestion,
Dataflow for processing,

00:32:45.830 --> 00:32:47.930
and for analysis, BigQuery.

00:32:47.930 --> 00:32:52.140
Again, no sharding, no capacity
guessing, no idle resources,

00:32:52.140 --> 00:32:53.240
et cetera.

00:32:53.240 --> 00:32:55.370
These are managed services.

00:32:55.370 --> 00:33:00.890
And so the BigQuery is
using standard SQL notation,

00:33:00.890 --> 00:33:03.380
so you don't have to go and
learn a new SQL dialect.

00:33:03.380 --> 00:33:06.850
There are a few add-ons for
unnesting data, for example.

00:33:06.850 --> 00:33:09.350
I'm sure that will be talked
about in the next conversation.

00:33:09.350 --> 00:33:12.030
I can show just that
very, very briefly here.

00:33:12.030 --> 00:33:13.970
So here is a row in the table.

00:33:13.970 --> 00:33:16.220
And here are the
nested rows, let's say.

00:33:16.220 --> 00:33:17.580
The repeated rows.

00:33:17.580 --> 00:33:21.500
So we have a few extensions
to deal with that.

00:33:21.500 --> 00:33:24.274
And Dataflow-- that all
important processing part--

00:33:24.274 --> 00:33:26.690
because this is where the magic
is happening, where you're

00:33:26.690 --> 00:33:29.330
actually transforming your data,
and filtering it, and applying

00:33:29.330 --> 00:33:30.600
all sorts of complicated stuff.

00:33:30.600 --> 00:33:32.141
This is where your
business logic is.

00:33:32.141 --> 00:33:33.740
You can write that Apache Beam.

00:33:33.740 --> 00:33:35.340
That's open source.

00:33:35.340 --> 00:33:37.799
You can run that on Spark,
you can run it on Flink,

00:33:37.799 --> 00:33:40.340
on your own Hadoop clusters--
as it's running at the moment--

00:33:40.340 --> 00:33:42.770
or if you run it by us, we'll
run it in a managed service

00:33:42.770 --> 00:33:43.750
and you won't have
to worry about all

00:33:43.750 --> 00:33:44.833
those things on the right.

00:33:48.050 --> 00:33:50.330
This line here is basically
saying a little bit

00:33:50.330 --> 00:33:53.090
about how much Google
has been involved

00:33:53.090 --> 00:33:57.230
in open source and Big
Data technology, so things

00:33:57.230 --> 00:33:58.940
like GFS and MapReduce.

00:33:58.940 --> 00:34:01.770
These are two white papers
from, I said, early 2000.

00:34:01.770 --> 00:34:03.219
There you go-- 2004.

00:34:03.219 --> 00:34:04.760
And these were
basically white papers

00:34:04.760 --> 00:34:11.600
that really kind of instigated
the whole Hadoop conversation,

00:34:11.600 --> 00:34:13.130
let's say.

00:34:13.130 --> 00:34:17.210
Our externalized version
of Dremel is BigQuery.

00:34:17.210 --> 00:34:20.179
Basically, we built
BigQuery because MapReduce

00:34:20.179 --> 00:34:22.469
wasn't fast enough, nor
was it friendly enough.

00:34:22.469 --> 00:34:25.280
So one of those things-- we
want to spend time on insights,

00:34:25.280 --> 00:34:28.010
we don't want to spend time
writing complicated pipelines,

00:34:28.010 --> 00:34:29.250
if we don't have to.

00:34:29.250 --> 00:34:32.600
So with BigQuery, you're
just using standard SQL.

00:34:32.600 --> 00:34:35.449
And you can run,
literally, a petabyte query

00:34:35.449 --> 00:34:36.830
in a couple of minutes.

00:34:36.830 --> 00:34:39.109
It's going to be an expensive
query because that's

00:34:39.109 --> 00:34:41.094
a lot of data to
process, but if you

00:34:41.094 --> 00:34:43.010
want to run a petabyte
in a couple of minutes,

00:34:43.010 --> 00:34:46.250
it's completely
possible with BigQuery.

00:34:46.250 --> 00:34:53.840
So another challenge
is silos of analytics.

00:34:53.840 --> 00:34:56.120
So how can we avoid those?

00:34:56.120 --> 00:34:57.950
Well, we can avoid
those like we avoid

00:34:57.950 --> 00:35:00.770
most things-- by separating
out responsibilities.

00:35:00.770 --> 00:35:05.340
In this case, separating
storage and compute.

00:35:05.340 --> 00:35:08.360
On the lower line here,
we have storage options--

00:35:08.360 --> 00:35:11.030
so cloud storage,
object storage files.

00:35:11.030 --> 00:35:12.140
We have BigQuery.

00:35:12.140 --> 00:35:14.348
And BigQuery, in this case--
when we're talking about

00:35:14.348 --> 00:35:15.180
BigQuery storage--

00:35:15.180 --> 00:35:17.960
so BigQuery has two parts--
the processing and the storage.

00:35:17.960 --> 00:35:22.190
And BigQuery storage is
really optimized storage.

00:35:22.190 --> 00:35:24.380
It's column optimized
storage, so you can really

00:35:24.380 --> 00:35:27.530
get great performance
from your queries.

00:35:27.530 --> 00:35:30.770
And the last one here
is Cloud Bigtable.

00:35:30.770 --> 00:35:32.769
This is our own, internal
version of what--

00:35:32.769 --> 00:35:34.310
in the external
version, for example,

00:35:34.310 --> 00:35:36.809
in the Hadoop community, you
would see something like HBase.

00:35:39.470 --> 00:35:42.030
Bigtable itself
isn't open source,

00:35:42.030 --> 00:35:46.520
but we do supply an API
which is HBase compliant.

00:35:46.520 --> 00:35:49.520
So if you would like to
move from Bigtable table

00:35:49.520 --> 00:35:52.340
into HBase, to running your own
HBase cluster in the future,

00:35:52.340 --> 00:35:53.630
you could do that completely.

00:35:53.630 --> 00:35:57.447
And we have an API, which
means that how you communicate

00:35:57.447 --> 00:35:59.030
with Bigtable would
be the same as how

00:35:59.030 --> 00:36:01.700
you communicate with HBase.

00:36:01.700 --> 00:36:03.410
So this is storage options.

00:36:03.410 --> 00:36:05.760
On the top line, we
have the processing.

00:36:05.760 --> 00:36:08.480
So we just looked at Dataflow.

00:36:08.480 --> 00:36:12.494
BigQuery analytics-- so this is
basically the part of BigQuery

00:36:12.494 --> 00:36:14.660
where you're actually writing
queries and processing

00:36:14.660 --> 00:36:15.980
your data.

00:36:15.980 --> 00:36:20.330
And Dataproc-- I don't have time
to speak about Dataproc today,

00:36:20.330 --> 00:36:24.080
but Dataproc is, basically, our
Spark and Hadoop, The Google

00:36:24.080 --> 00:36:25.640
Cloud way.

00:36:25.640 --> 00:36:29.270
You can spin up a cluster
in under 90 seconds.

00:36:29.270 --> 00:36:32.980
You can, basically, start
thinking of MapReduce,

00:36:32.980 --> 00:36:36.160
and Spark jobs, and Hive
jobs, or Pig scripts

00:36:36.160 --> 00:36:39.176
in the terms of a job
as opposed to a cluster.

00:36:39.176 --> 00:36:40.300
You're built by the minute.

00:36:40.300 --> 00:36:42.910
Because we can spin up
and tear down so quickly,

00:36:42.910 --> 00:36:45.520
it completely changes
how you look at that.

00:36:45.520 --> 00:36:47.170
So if you're running
Hadoop at scale

00:36:47.170 --> 00:36:49.210
and you're interested
to know more about this,

00:36:49.210 --> 00:36:53.620
please contact any of us
or any of our partners.

00:36:53.620 --> 00:36:56.000
We'd be happy to talk
you through that.

00:36:56.000 --> 00:36:57.375
In all three of
these cases here,

00:36:57.375 --> 00:36:58.791
what we're basically
doing-- we're

00:36:58.791 --> 00:37:00.514
giving the options to
be able to interact

00:37:00.514 --> 00:37:01.930
with any of these
processing units

00:37:01.930 --> 00:37:05.740
with any of the storage units
underneath, as you can see.

00:37:05.740 --> 00:37:07.300
So that really gives
you the ability

00:37:07.300 --> 00:37:09.040
to kind of separate where
you're storing things

00:37:09.040 --> 00:37:11.331
and make it available for
all your different processing

00:37:11.331 --> 00:37:13.700
environments.

00:37:13.700 --> 00:37:15.540
Crowd ML engine--

